{
  "id": "derangements-oq-03-oq-01-oq-02",
  "title": "Closed-Form Error for Derangements as an Alternating Tail Sum (OQ-03-OQ-01-OQ-02)",
  "description": "Can the error D(n)/n! - 1/e be expressed as a closed-form alternating sum, not just bounded by 1/(n+1)!? The answer is yes: the error equals the negation of the alternating series tail starting at n+1.",
  "source": {
    "proofId": "derangements-oq-03-oq-01",
    "proofTitle": "Second-Order Derangement Convergence Rate",
    "type": "open-question"
  },
  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "Express D(n) / n! - exp(-1) as an explicit alternating tail sum and derive the error sign pattern.",
    "plain": "Strengthen the derangements convergence story by replacing a bare error bound with an exact alternating-series tail formula.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "The exact tail formula explains both the size and sign of the derangement approximation error.",
      "It gives reusable lemmas for sharper convergence estimates around D(n) / n! -> 1 / e."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "errorTailSum defines the alternating factorial tail used in the exact error formula.",
      "errorTailSum_nonneg, errorTailSum_le, errorTailSum_pos, and errorTailSum_ge bound the alternating tail.",
      "error_closed_form expresses D(n) / n! - exp(-1) as a signed tail sum.",
      "error_recurrence gives a recurrence for the exact approximation error.",
      "error_strict_pos_even and error_strict_neg_odd prove the even/odd sign pattern.",
      "normalized_error_tendsto proves the normalized error tends to 1."
    ],
    "open": [],
    "goal": "Make the exact derangement-error formula and sign behavior visible in the research metadata."
  },
